---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import type { ArticleMeta } from '../../types/article';

export const meta: ArticleMeta = {
  title: 'Pink PS5 Controller: Every Pink DualSense Option & Which to Buy (2026)',
  description:
    'Looking for a pink PS5 controller? We compare Sony\'s Nova Pink DualSense against Cosmic Red, Chroma Pearl, and third-party pink pads to help you pick the right one.',
  category: 'controllers',
  type: 'review',
  keyword: 'pink ps5 controller',
  date: '2026-09-21',
  updated: '2026-09-21',
  image: '/images/placeholder.svg',
  featured: true,
  rating: 4.7,
  price: 79.99,
  affiliateUrl: 'https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pink+ps5+controller',
  author: 'Lucas Hart',
  authorBio:
    'Lucas Hart has spent five years testing PlayStation hardware, from hall effect controllers to SSD storage upgrades, and writes hands-on buying guides for Gearky.',
};
---

<ArticleLayout
  meta={meta}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'What is Sony\'s pink PS5 controller called?',
      a: 'The official pink DualSense is called Nova Pink. It is the only true pink in Sony\'s first-party lineup. If Nova Pink is out of stock, Cosmic Red (a deep red) and Chroma Pearl (a pearl white with a pink tint) are the closest alternatives.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How much is the pink PS5 controller?',
      a: 'The Nova Pink DualSense lists for $79.99 in the US, the same as other colored DualSense models. The standard white and Midnight Black pads cost $74.99, so you pay a $5 premium for the color.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does the pink controller work on PC and mobile?',
      a: 'Yes. Like every DualSense, Nova Pink works on PS5, PC (wired or Bluetooth), and mobile devices. Haptic feedback and adaptive triggers work fully on PS5; support on PC varies by game.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is Nova Pink discontinued?',
      a: 'Colored DualSense models are released in waves and stock can be spotty, so Nova Pink has periodically gone out of stock. It has not been formally discontinued, but availability changes, which is why we link to current listings below.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Are third-party pink controllers as good as the DualSense?',
      a: 'It depends on what you want. A third-party pink pad (from Scuf, PDP, or PowerA) can offer extra features like remappable buttons or longer battery life, but most drop the DualSense\'s haptic feedback and adaptive triggers. For the full PS5 feature set, stick with Nova Pink.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Do colored DualSense controllers have different internals?',
      a: 'No. Nova Pink is cosmetically identical to the standard DualSense — same haptics, adaptive triggers, mic, speaker, and battery. You are only paying for the color.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    If you want a pink PS5 controller, Sony&apos;s <strong>Nova Pink DualSense</strong> is the
    obvious first choice — it is the only true pink in the official lineup, and it
    carries every feature that makes the DualSense one of the best controllers ever
    made. But "pink" means different things to different people, and Sony has released
    several red and pearl shades that people often group under the same search. Here is
    exactly what is available, how the shades differ, and which pink pad is actually
    worth your money.
  </p>

  <h2>The official pink: Nova Pink DualSense</h2>

  <p>
    Nova Pink is a soft, true pink — closer to a muted rose than a loud neon — and it is
    the only first-party DualSense sold as "pink." Under the shell it is the exact same
    controller as the white original, which is a good thing. You get the full feature
    set: <strong>haptic feedback</strong> that produces detailed, positional rumble,
    <strong>adaptive triggers</strong> that change resistance on the fly, a built-in
    microphone and speaker, a touchpad, motion controls, and a 3.5mm headphone jack.
  </p>

  <p>
    The color itself is the reason to buy it, and it holds up well in person — the matte
    finish resists fingerprints better than the glossy black sections on some models.
    It pairs cleanly with a white console and looks especially good next to the
    matching Nova Pink console covers if you want to theme the whole setup.
  </p>

  <figure class="article-figure">
    <img
      src="/images/pink-controller-colors.svg"
      alt="Comparison of Sony pink and red DualSense colors: Nova Pink, Cosmic Red, Chroma Pearl, Volcanic Red"
      loading="lazy"
      width="800"
      height="450"
    />
    <figcaption>Nova Pink is the only true pink. Cosmic Red, Chroma Pearl, and Volcanic Red are the closest alternatives.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>If you want a different shade of "pink"</h2>

  <p>
    A lot of people searching for a pink controller actually want one of the nearby
    shades. Here is how they differ so you do not order the wrong color:
  </p>

  <ul class="shade-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Cosmic Red:</strong> A deep, wine-toned red. It is striking and premium
      looking, but it reads red, not pink. Choose this if Nova Pink feels too light.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Chroma Pearl:</strong> Part of the Chroma series, a pearl finish that
      shifts between soft pink, cream, and lavender depending on the light. It is the
      subtlest "pink-adjacent" option and a favorite for a clean aesthetic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Volcanic Red:</strong> A bold, saturated red with no pink in it. Only
      consider this if you want maximum vibrancy rather than a pastel look.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Starlight Blue &amp; Galactic Purple:</strong> Not pink at all, but they are
      the other pastel-leaning options if you are shopping by vibe rather than hue.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What every pink DualSense includes</h2>

  <p>
    Because the internals are identical across colors, the feature set is the same no
    matter which shade you pick. These are the two features that matter most in real
    gameplay:
  </p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Haptic feedback:</strong> Instead of a single rumble motor, the DualSense
      uses voice-coil actuators that produce distinct sensations — the crunch of gravel,
      the patter of rain, the recoil of a shotgun. It is a level of immersion older
      controllers cannot match.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Adaptive triggers:</strong> The L2 and R2 triggers physically resist your
      finger depending on the action — drawing a bow feels different from pulling a
      trigger, and pedals can stiffen in racing games. It sounds gimmicky, but it is
      genuinely the standout feature of this generation.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <figure class="article-figure">
    <img
      src="/images/pink-controller-features.svg"
      alt="DualSense features: haptic feedback, adaptive triggers, built-in mic and speaker, motion and touchpad"
      loading="lazy"
      width="800"
      height="450"
    />
    <figcaption>Every DualSense, including Nova Pink, ships with the same haptics, triggers, mic, and motion controls.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Third-party pink controllers</h2>

  <p>
    If you want something the official pad cannot offer — remappable back buttons, a
    longer battery, or a specific custom look — there are third-party pink controllers
    worth knowing about. Scuf, PDP, and PowerA all sell pink or customizable pads, and
    some (like the Victrix Pro BFG or Scuf Reflex) add pro-style features. The trade-off
    is almost always the same: most third-party pads drop the DualSense&apos;s haptic
    feedback and adaptive triggers, so you gain customization but lose immersion. For
    the vast majority of players, the official Nova Pink is the better buy.
  </p>

  <h2>Pricing and compatibility</h2>

  <p>
    In the US, the Nova Pink DualSense lists for <strong>$79.99</strong>, versus $74.99 for
    the standard white and Midnight Black models — a $5 premium for the color. It works
    wirelessly with the PS5, and on PC and mobile over Bluetooth or USB, though the
    advanced haptics are a PS5-first experience.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>

  <p>
    The pink PS5 controller to buy is the <strong>Nova Pink DualSense</strong>. It is the
    only genuine pink in Sony&apos;s lineup, it costs just $5 more than the standard pad,
    and it delivers the full haptic and adaptive-trigger experience that makes the
    DualSense special. Only look at Cosmic Red, Chroma Pearl, or a third-party pad if
    you want a different shade or pro-style extras. For most people, Nova Pink is the
    answer — check the latest price below and grab one while it is in stock.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .shade-list,
  .feature-list {
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
  }
  .shade-list li,
  .feature-list li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  .article-figure {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .article-figure img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .article-figure figcaption {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    text-align: center;
  }
</style>
